exports[`renderPortainerRunbook > tunnel, updater, an R2 bucket: the runbook stays as it was 1`] = `
"# Runbook: shop

Generated by OpsCrew 0.1.0 from \`opscrew/plan.yaml\`. Run \`opscrew verify\` to refresh it.

## At a glance

| | |
|---|---|
| App | https://shop.example.com |
| Health check | \`/api/health\` |
| Runs on | Your Portainer, environment 3 (amd64), as the stack \`opscrew-shop\` |
| Visitors | Through a Cloudflare Tunnel: the cloudflared container in \`opscrew-shop\` connects out to Cloudflare, and the stack publishes no port on the host. |
| Deploys | every push to \`main\` runs \`.github/workflows/opscrew-deploy.yml\` |
| Image | \`ghcr.io/acme/shop\` (GitHub Container Registry) |
| Database | PostgreSQL in the app's stack, data in the volume \`opscrew-shop_db_data\` |

## How a change reaches your Portainer

- Every push to \`main\` runs \`.github/workflows/opscrew-deploy.yml\`: it builds the image, pushes it to GitHub Container Registry and writes \`compose.yaml\` with the new image to the \`opscrew-deploy\` branch.
- The updater stack \`opscrew-shop-updater\` asks your Portainer once a minute, over the local network, to redeploy \`opscrew-shop\` from that branch when it changed.
- The workflow reports success only once the site answers its health check with the new version; otherwise it writes the previous image back to the \`opscrew-deploy\` branch, so the previous version returns.

## Rolling back

Re-run an earlier successful run of \`.github/workflows/opscrew-deploy.yml\` in GitHub Actions: it writes that run's image to the \`opscrew-deploy\` branch, and your Portainer deploys it.

## Components

The app runs as the stack \`opscrew-shop\` in your Portainer. Each component is a container with its own memory
limit, and none publishes a port.

| Component | Runs | Memory limit | Data |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`app\` | the web process | 512 MB | |
| \`db\` | PostgreSQL 17 (PostgreSQL) | 512 MB | the Docker volume \`opscrew-shop_db_data\` |

\`opscrew status\` shows whether each component runs, and Portainer shows each container's log. Change a
component's count, memory or version, or turn one off, in \`opscrew/plan.yaml\`, then run \`opscrew design\` and
\`opscrew provision\`.

## Object storage

The app's files are in the private bucket \`shop-uploads\` on Cloudflare R2 (auto), about
€0.00 a month for 10 GB stored and 50 GB sent a month (from test, checked on 2026-10-19). OpsCrew creates the bucket in its own root, \`opscrew/infra/storage/\`, so \`opscrew destroy\` keeps it
with its files unless you add \`--delete-data\`.

- **The storage key is yours.** OpsCrew never creates one: you created it in the provider's console with the steps
  \`opscrew provision\` showed. OpsCrew keeps it on the machine that ran \`opscrew provision\`
  (\`~/.opscrew/projects/shop-0123456789ab/storage_key.json\`), never in this repository or in infrastructure state, and the app gets it
  as \`AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID\` and \`AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY\`, with \`AWS_REGION\` and \`AWS_ENDPOINT_URL_S3\`.
- **To replace it:** create a new key, delete the stored copy (or set \`OPSCREW_STORAGE_ACCESS_KEY_ID\` and
  \`OPSCREW_STORAGE_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY\` to it in scripts), run \`opscrew provision\` to give it to the app, re-run the
  deploy workflow, then revoke the old key in the provider's console.

## Logs and status

In your Portainer: Stacks → \`opscrew-shop\` → a container → Logs. \`opscrew verify\` checks the site from outside, \`opscrew drift\` compares the stacks with what OpsCrew sets up, and \`opscrew secure\` checks the app and its stack.

## Secrets

The app's values live only in the stack's environment in your Portainer, set through its API from the computer that ran \`opscrew provision\`; they are never in the repository, the pull requests or GitHub Actions.
To change one, update the production values and run \`opscrew provision\`: its stack plan names the variables that change (never their values). After rotating the repository or registry token, run \`opscrew provision --replace-token repository\` or \`--replace-token registry\`.

## The secrets recovery copy

\`opscrew/secrets.sops.json\` is an encrypted copy of what OpsCrew keeps for this app on the machine that ran
\`opscrew provision\`: the generated passwords and keys, the deploy key, the infrastructure state and its passphrase,
and OpsCrew's records. Only your age key (or your backup key) opens it, and OpsCrew keeps no copy of the key.
\`opscrew provision\` updates it in its pull request whenever one of those files changes; \`opscrew secrets status\`
shows when it was last updated and whether it is behind.

To manage the deployment from another computer:

\`\`\`bash
git clone <this repository> && cd <it>
opscrew secrets restore --key-file <your age key file>   # or without --key-file, to paste the key unseen
opscrew provision                                        # reuses the restored files: nothing is generated again
\`\`\`

- **Without OpsCrew:** \`SOPS_AGE_KEY_FILE=<your age key file> sops decrypt opscrew/secrets.sops.json\` prints the files.
- **A new key:** \`opscrew secrets rotate --key-out <file>\` writes a new key to that file and proposes the copy,
  encrypted to it, in a pull request. Older commits still open with the old key: if it leaked, change the secrets
  themselves too.

## What OpsCrew does not do here

- OpsCrew does not change the host: its firewall, SSH and Portainer's own security stay yours.
- Backups, including the app's data volumes, are your responsibility: OpsCrew cannot back up a Portainer host. This app's volumes: \`opscrew-shop_db_data\`. \`opscrew-shop_version\` holds only the version the site serves: nothing to back up.
- OpsCrew changes nothing in your Portainer that it did not create: other stacks, volumes, registry entries and Portainer's settings stay yours.

## Removing the app

\`opscrew destroy\` removes the app's stacks, the registry entry OpsCrew added, the deploy secrets and the app's Cloudflare resources, after approvals and a typed confirmation of the app's name. The app's volumes and its bucket \`shop-uploads\` stay unless you add \`--delete-data\`.
"
